      "dom_id": "question-37821",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243863/assets/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243863/assets/438ce908c902696b480bf9791cae089be40f9e7b2552208e13e08405496b696c.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$",
