      "dom_id": "question-65061",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243863/assets/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243863/assets/e2c83b965f7108bff3165531446cce43006a41b9742914e8fc40c50741230427.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$",
